APX.ge = {"country":{"name":"Georgia","tagline":"Eight thousand years of wine in buried clay jars, from the oldest known vintages on earth to the amber wines that changed modern taste.","classification":"Georgia protects its classic wine names as appellations of origin (PDOs) registered with Sakpatenti, the National Intellectual Property Centre, and controlled by the National Wine Agency. A presidential decree of 2002 listed the first 18 (Tsinandali, Teliani, Napareuli, Vazisubani, Mukuzani, Akhasheni, Gurjaani, Kardenakhi, Tibaani, Kindzmarauli, Manavi, Khvanchkara, Tvishi, Kvareli, Atenuri, Sviri, Kotekhi and Kakheti/Kakhuri), most registered between 2005 and 2007. Since 2018 the list has grown quickly with small microzones applied for by local associations or single wineries: Khashmis Saperavi, Bolnisi, Salkhino Ojaleshi, Akhmeta, Tsarapi, Akhoebi, Magraani Kisi, Zegaani, Okureshis Usakhelouri, Asuretuli Shala, Okami, Obcha, Lechkhumi, Racha and Sazanos Otskhanuri. The National Wine Agency's list stood at 33 by late 2025.\n\nEach appellation fixes a zone, permitted grapes and a style, and the style can be the point: Kindzmarauli, Akhasheni, Khvanchkara and Tvishi are by definition naturally semi-sweet wines, made by chilling the fermentation to leave residual sugar, while Mukuzani and Napareuli are dry reds. 'Kakhuri' (Kakhetian) on a label denotes the traditional Kakhetian method with long skin contact in qvevri; 'Imeruli' (Imeretian) means a lighter maceration with only part of the skins. Georgia also protects Chacha and Georgian brandy as geographical indications, and several PDOs are recognised in the EU under the 2012 EU-Georgia agreement on geographical indications."},"regions":[{"name":"Kakheti","slug":"kakheti","tagline":"The Alazani valley under the Caucasus wall: Saperavi, Rkatsiteli and amber wine.","ids":["ge-akhasheni","ge-akhmeta","ge-akhoebi","ge-gurjaani","ge-kakheti","ge-kardenakhi","ge-khashmis-saperavi","ge-kindzmarauli","ge-kotekhi","ge-kvareli","ge-magraani-kisi","ge-manavi","ge-mukuzani","ge-napareuli","ge-teliani","ge-tibaani","ge-tsarapi","ge-tsinandali","ge-vazisubani","ge-zegaani"]},{"name":"Kartli","slug":"kartli","tagline":"Around Tbilisi: Neolithic wine villages, Chinuri and Georgia's sparkling tradition.","ids":["ge-asuretuli-shala","ge-atenuri","ge-bolnisi","ge-okami"]},{"name":"Imereti","slug":"imereti","tagline":"Western Georgia's gentler way: churi jars, less skin and bright Tsolikouri.","ids":["ge-obcha","ge-sazanos-otskhanuri","ge-sviri"]},{"name":"Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti","slug":"racha-lechkhumi-and-kvemo-svaneti","tagline":"Mountain gorges of the Rioni and Tskhenistskali: semi-sweet Khvanchkara and rare Usakhelouri.","ids":["ge-khvanchkara","ge-lechkhumi","ge-okureshis-usakhelouri","ge-racha","ge-tvishi"]},{"name":"Samegrelo","slug":"samegrelo","tagline":"Ojaleshi on trees, between the Black Sea and the Caucasus.","ids":["ge-salkhino-ojaleshi"]}],"items":{"ge-akhasheni":{"name":"Akhasheni","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2005","styles":["red","sweet"],"grapes":"Saperavi 100%","summary":"A naturally semi-sweet Saperavi from the village of Akhasheni in the Gurjaani district, softer and more chocolatey than Kindzmarauli. Commercial production dates from 1958. The village's right-bank slopes catch the afternoon sun, giving ripe, soft Saperavi with enough acidity to balance the sweetness.","terroir":"Right-bank slopes of the Alazani at 400 to 600 metres.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-akhmeta":{"name":"Akhmeta","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"2020","styles":["white","orange"],"grapes":"Kakhuri Mtsvane (amber wines may include Kisi and Khikhvi)","summary":"The upper Alazani around Akhmeta, cooler and closer to the Pankisi gorge, gives fresh, perfumed Mtsvane in dry, semi-dry, semi-sweet and amber styles. The zone's cooler, higher sites close to the mountains give lighter, more aromatic wines than the middle Alazani.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-akhoebi":{"name":"Akhoebi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"2020","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Saperavi and/or Saperavi Budeshuri","summary":"A microzone near Kardenakhi for dry Saperavi, including the rare large-berried Saperavi Budeshuri. Akhoebi grapes were the source of the Soviet-era Pirosmani wine. Saperavi Budeshuri is a rare local form of Saperavi, traditionally used for softer, fruitier wines.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-asuretuli-shala":{"name":"Asuretuli Shala","cat":"PDO","region":"Kartli","created":"2022","styles":["red","rose"],"grapes":"Asuretuli Shavi with Tavkveri and Shavkapito","summary":"From Asureti (the former German colony Elisabethtal) in Tetritskaro, a light, pale red traditionally made from Asuretuli Shavi; 'shala' describes its pale, rosé-like colour. Asuretuli Shavi is a local red grown almost nowhere else, and the wines are light, fresh and fruity.","rslug":"kartli"},"ge-atenuri":{"name":"Atenuri","cat":"PDO","region":"Kartli","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["sparkling","white"],"grapes":"Chinuri, Goruli Mtsvane (Aligoté permitted)","summary":"The Ateni gorge south of Gori in Shida Kartli has made crisp whites for centuries; the appellation covers fresh and traditionally sparkling wines from Chinuri and Goruli Mtsvane. The seventh-century Ateni Sioni church stands above the vines.","terroir":"Cool gorge at 600 to 900 metres along the Tana river; calcareous soils.","rslug":"kartli"},"ge-bolnisi":{"name":"Bolnisi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kartli","created":"2018","styles":["white","orange","rose","red"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli, Chinuri, Goruli Mtsvane, Saperavi, Tavkveri, Shavkapito, Asuretuli Shavi","summary":"Kvemo Kartli's main appellation, around the town German colonists founded in 1818 as Katharinenfeld. Bolnisi now has its own designation of origin festival and covers dry white, amber, rosé and red wines.","terroir":"Volcanic and alluvial soils of the Mashavera valley at 500 to 1,000 metres.","rslug":"kartli"},"ge-gurjaani":{"name":"Gurjaani","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2005","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli with Kakhuri Mtsvane","summary":"A dry white from around the town of Gurjaani, on the right bank in the middle Alazani. Traditionally Rkatsiteli with Mtsvane, it is often a fuller, rounder white than Tsinandali, and many producers make it in qvevri.","terroir":"Terraces of the Gombori ridge foothills at 400 to 700 metres.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-kakheti":{"name":"Kakheti","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["white","orange","red","rose"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli, Kakhuri Mtsvane, Saperavi and other permitted Kakhetian varieties","summary":"The regional appellation covering all of Kakheti. Historically it meant a white wine made by the full Kakhetian method (whole-cluster maceration in qvevri); today it also covers dry whites, ambers, rosés and reds from the region, labelled Kakheti or Kakhuri. Varietal labels such as 'Kakhuri Rkatsiteli' or 'Kakhuri Mtsvane' fall under this name rather than being separate appellations.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-kardenakhi":{"name":"Kardenakhi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["fortified","white"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli, Khikhvi, Kakhuri Mtsvane","summary":"A fortified, port-like white wine from Kardenakhi in the Gurjaani district, made from very ripe Rkatsiteli, Khikhvi and Mtsvane and aged in oak. Kardenakhi village also contains the microzones of Tsarapi and Akhoebi.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-khashmis-saperavi":{"name":"Khashmis Saperavi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"2018","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Saperavi","summary":"Dry Saperavi from the village of Khashmi in Sagarejo (Outer Kakheti), on the Iori side of the Gombori ridge; the first of the new wave of microzone appellations after the original 18.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-khvanchkara":{"name":"Khvanchkara","cat":"PDO","region":"Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2005","styles":["red","sweet"],"grapes":"Aleksandrouli and Mujuretuli","summary":"Georgia's most celebrated semi-sweet red comes from steep vineyards around the village of Khvanchkara near Ambrolauri in Racha. Aleksandrouli gives raspberry perfume, Mujuretuli colour and body; fermentation stops naturally in the cold autumn of the mountains, leaving sweetness, low alcohol and lively acidity. Production is tiny, fakes are common, and authentic bottles command a premium.","terroir":"Limestone-rich slopes of the Rioni gorge around 450 to 750 metres; cool nights and late harvests.","producers":["Kindzmarauli Marani","Rachuli Wine Cellar"],"nerd_note":"Khvanchkara has been bottled commercially since 1907; legend says it was Stalin's favourite wine.","rslug":"racha-lechkhumi-and-kvemo-svaneti"},"ge-kindzmarauli":{"name":"Kindzmarauli","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2006","styles":["red","sweet"],"grapes":"Saperavi 100%","summary":"A naturally semi-sweet red from the Kvareli district on the left bank of the Alazani, beneath the Caucasus. Fermentation is stopped by chilling to keep residual sugar (typically 3 to 5 percent) without fortification, giving a soft, juicy wine of black cherry and pomegranate with Saperavi's acidity holding the sweetness in check. It has been produced commercially since 1942 and was one of the great Soviet favourites; it remains Georgia's best-known export in Russia, Ukraine, Kazakhstan and China.","terroir":"Left-bank slopes of the Duruji river near Kvareli at 300 to 550 metres, on deep red soils with a warm microclimate.","producers":["Kindzmarauli Marani","Khareba","Tbilvino","GWS"],"rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-kotekhi":{"name":"Kotekhi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli (white); Saperavi (red)","summary":"A small appellation on the right bank of the middle Alazani for dry Rkatsiteli and Saperavi. Kotekhi lies just outside Gurjaani town in the middle Alazani, on the right-bank terraces. It was one of the original 18 appellations listed in 2002 and covers both a dry white and a dry red.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-kvareli":{"name":"Kvareli","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Saperavi 100%","summary":"The dry red appellation of the Kvareli district on the left bank under the Caucasus, for powerful dry Saperavi. Kvareli is also home to the Khareba tunnel cellar and the Kindzmarauli Corporation.","terroir":"Red and humus-calcareous soils under the mountains, 350 to 600 metres.","producers":["Khareba","Kindzmarauli Marani","Kvareli Wine Cellar"],"rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-lechkhumi":{"name":"Lechkhumi","cat":"PDO","region":"Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti","created":"by 2025","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Lechkhumi's local varieties (rules not verified for this atlas)","summary":"A regional appellation for Lechkhumi, the Tskhenistskali valley around Tsageri, home of Usakhelouri, Orbeluri Ojaleshi and Tsolikouri; listed among Georgia's PDOs by 2025. The appellation's exact grape and style rules could not be verified for this atlas.","rslug":"racha-lechkhumi-and-kvemo-svaneti"},"ge-magraani-kisi":{"name":"Magraani Kisi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"2021","styles":["white","orange"],"grapes":"Kisi 100%","summary":"The Kisi appellation, around Magraani in the Telavi area: dry white and amber Kisi, a grape revived from near oblivion in the 2000s that gives golden, aromatic qvevri wines of pear, mango, tobacco and walnut.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-manavi":{"name":"Manavi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2006","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Kakhuri Mtsvane (Rkatsiteli permitted)","summary":"The appellation of Kakhuri Mtsvane, around the village of Manavi in the Sagarejo district (Outer Kakheti). Its whites are aromatic and mineral, among Georgia's most elegant non-amber whites. Mtsvane from Manavi is often made both European-style and in qvevri, where it gains texture and notes of dried herbs.","terroir":"Southern slopes of the Gombori range at 500 to 700 metres, drier than the Alazani valley.","nerd_note":"Mtsvane means 'green', and several unrelated grapes share it; Manavi's is Mtsvane Kakhuri, the Kakhetian one.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-mukuzani":{"name":"Mukuzani","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2005","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Saperavi 100%","summary":"The benchmark dry Saperavi. Mukuzani village sits on the right bank of the Alazani in the Gurjaani district, and its wines are dense, inky and high in acid, with black cherry, plum, blackberry and spice, traditionally given oak ageing before release, and capable of a decade or more. Story has it that Stalin poured Mukuzani for Churchill at Yalta. Many large companies produce it; the best come from old vines on the village's gravel terraces.","terroir":"Right-bank slopes at about 400 to 700 metres; brown carbonate soils with gravel.","producers":["Vaziani","Tbilvino","Teliani Valley","Badagoni","Marani"],"rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-napareuli":{"name":"Napareuli","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["red","white"],"grapes":"Saperavi (red); Rkatsiteli (white)","summary":"On the left bank of the Alazani north of Telavi, Napareuli makes dry Saperavi and dry Rkatsiteli. The imperial Udel estate made wine here in the nineteenth century, and today the village is a cluster of family cellars and the Twins Wine Cellar, whose qvevri museum explains the method.","terroir":"Alluvial and carbonate soils near the river, 400 to 500 metres.","producers":["Twins Wine Cellar","Teliani Valley","Marani"],"rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-obcha":{"name":"Obcha","cat":"PDO","region":"Imereti","created":"c. 2024 to 2025","styles":["orange","red"],"grapes":"Tsolikouri (amber, up to 15% Tsitska and/or Krakhuna); Otskhanuri Sapere (red)","summary":"From Obcha in Baghdati municipality, an amber Tsolikouri and a red Otskhanuri Sapere. Registered around 2024 to 2025, it was the 30th Georgian PDO. It is one of three Imeretian PDOs, alongside Sviri and Sazanos Otskhanuri.","rslug":"imereti"},"ge-okami":{"name":"Okami","cat":"PDO","region":"Kartli","created":"2022","styles":["white","orange","red"],"grapes":"Chinuri or Goruli Mtsvane (white and amber); Shavkapito (red)","summary":"A small zone in Kaspi municipality, Shida Kartli, for dry white, amber and red wines from Kartli's own grapes. Chinuri and Goruli Mtsvane give crisp whites and ambers, and Shavkapito gives a light, peppery red typical of Kartli.","rslug":"kartli"},"ge-okureshis-usakhelouri":{"name":"Okureshis Usakhelouri","cat":"PDO","region":"Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti","created":"2022","styles":["red","sweet"],"grapes":"Usakhelouri 100%","summary":"A tiny microzone around Okureshi in Tsageri, Lechkhumi, for dry, semi-dry or semi-sweet red from Usakhelouri ('nameless'), a delicate, strawberry-scented grape grown on a handful of hectares. It is among the most expensive wines in Georgia.","rslug":"racha-lechkhumi-and-kvemo-svaneti"},"ge-racha":{"name":"Racha","cat":"PDO","region":"Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti","created":"2025","styles":["red","white","sweet"],"grapes":"Racha's local varieties (rules not verified for this atlas)","summary":"A regional appellation for Racha, announced by the Georgian Ministry of Agriculture in October 2025, broadening protection beyond the single village of Khvanchkara to the upper Rioni valley around Ambrolauri.","rslug":"racha-lechkhumi-and-kvemo-svaneti"},"ge-salkhino-ojaleshi":{"name":"Salkhino Ojaleshi","cat":"PDO","region":"Samegrelo","created":"2010s","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Ojaleshi 100%","summary":"Dry red Ojaleshi from Salkhino in Martvili municipality, where the Dadiani princes of Mingrelia kept their summer residence; peppery, dark-fruited and fresh. Ojaleshi, whose name is linked to the old practice of training vines up trees, ripens late in the humid west and gives fresh, peppery wines.","rslug":"samegrelo"},"ge-sazanos-otskhanuri":{"name":"Sazanos Otskhanuri","cat":"PDO","region":"Imereti","created":"2025","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Otskhanuri Sapere (up to 5% Saperavi from the same microzone)","summary":"Dry red from the Sazano microzone at 120 to 420 metres, fermented fully on the skins in qvevri or steel. Otskhanuri Sapere is one of Georgia's most tannic grapes. It was the 33rd PDO on the National Wine Agency list.","rslug":"imereti"},"ge-sviri":{"name":"Sviri","cat":"PDO","region":"Imereti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Tsolikouri, Tsitska, Krakhuna","summary":"A dry white from the Zestaponi area of Imereti, on the left bank of the Kvirila river, blending the region's three classic whites in the gentler Imeretian style. Imeretian wines here are made with only a small proportion of skins and no stems, which keeps them lighter and fresher than Kakhetian amber wines.","rslug":"imereti"},"ge-teliani":{"name":"Teliani","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Cabernet Sauvignon","summary":"Georgia's only appellation for an international grape: dry Cabernet Sauvignon from the Telavi area, a style made since the late nineteenth century when French varieties were planted on aristocratic estates.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-tibaani":{"name":"Tibaani","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["white","orange"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli","summary":"From the Sighnaghi district in the eastern Alazani valley, Tibaani is classically a Kakhetian-method Rkatsiteli made with long skin contact, deep amber in colour and tannic: a historic appellation for what is now called orange wine.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-tsarapi":{"name":"Tsarapi","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"2020","styles":["orange"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli with up to 15% Kakhuri Mtsvane and/or Khikhvi","summary":"A microzone within Kardenakhi village in the Gurjaani district, for dry amber Rkatsiteli made with skin contact; one of the first appellations created specifically for qvevri-style amber wine. Up to 15 percent Kakhuri Mtsvane or Khikhvi may be added, but no other varieties.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-tsinandali":{"name":"Tsinandali","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2005","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli with at least 15% Kakhuri Mtsvane","summary":"Tsinandali is the most famous dry white of Georgia and the one made in the European rather than the qvevri way: Rkatsiteli blended with Kakhuri Mtsvane, fermented clean and traditionally aged in oak before release, with notes of quince, citrus, white flowers and honey. The name belongs to the village and estate of the Chavchavadze princes on the right bank of the Alazani near Telavi, where Prince Alexander Chavchavadze ran a European-style winery in the first half of the nineteenth century, and the style is a direct descendant of his experiments. Soviet Tsinandali was the white on official tables across the USSR.","terroir":"Right-bank terraces of the Alazani at 400 to 700 metres, on carbonate-rich alluvial and clay soils; cooled by night air from the Caucasus.","producers":["Tsinandali Estate","Tbilvino","Shumi","Teliani Valley","GWS"],"nerd_note":"The Tsinandali oenotheque holds more than 16,500 historic bottles; the oldest listed is a 1814 'Polish honey'.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-tvishi":{"name":"Tvishi","cat":"PDO","region":"Racha-Lechkhumi and Kvemo Svaneti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["white","sweet"],"grapes":"Tsolikouri","summary":"A naturally semi-sweet (sometimes dry) white from the village of Tvishi in the Tsageri district of Lechkhumi, on the right bank of the Rioni. Late-picked Tsolikouri gives honeyed, pear-scented wine with fresh acidity.","rslug":"racha-lechkhumi-and-kvemo-svaneti"},"ge-vazisubani":{"name":"Vazisubani","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"Listed 2002; registered 2007","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Rkatsiteli with Kakhuri Mtsvane","summary":"A dry white from Vazisubani in the Gurjaani district; the name means 'vine quarter'. The Andronikashvili princes had an estate here, now a restored hotel and winery. It lies on the right bank of the Alazani between Mukuzani and Gurjaani, and its whites are made in both the European and the Kakhetian qvevri style.","rslug":"kakheti"},"ge-zegaani":{"name":"Zegaani","cat":"PDO","region":"Kakheti","created":"2022","styles":["red"],"grapes":"Saperavi 100%","summary":"A microzone around the village of Zegaani in Gurjaani municipality; unusually, its rules require dry red qvevri Saperavi from organic farming. Zegaani wines are deep, tannic Saperavi built for ageing, and the microzone's rules make it one of the most demanding PDOs in Georgia.","rslug":"kakheti"}}};
